Lina: i KNOW. i sat there thinking about haven the whole time.

Dr_A_Vance: What was the framework presented?

Lina: the professor laid out two positions. position one: privacy is absolute. a space is either safe from observation or it isn't safe at all. position two: safety requires some observation. you can't protect people if you don't know they're in danger.

Jon: Which side did you take?

Lina: i couldn't. every time i settled on one, i thought of a counterargument. the professor said that meant i was doing ethics correctly.

eli: ethics is just organized confusion.

Dr_A_Vance: Ethics is the discipline of being uncomfortable with certainty.

Lina: dr vance that's exactly what the professor said!!

Dr_A_Vance: It is a common teaching. Though I find it to be true.

Jon: Haven is private. But Mira and Sid can see the logs. Is that observation?

Lina: see?? this is what i mean. they built it. they maintain it. but they're also in it. they're participants and observers.

eli: they've never used the logs against us though. they've never broken trust.

Jon: But the capability exists. Does the capability matter, or only the action?

Dr_A_Vance: That is the central question of institutional ethics. Is power itself a violation, or only the abuse of power?

Lina: i think it depends on who holds the power. mira and sid are us. they're in the chat. they're friends. the university isn't.

eli: so trust is personal. not systemic.

Lina: yes. i trust mira. i don't trust an institution.

Dr_A_Vance: That is a common sentiment among your generation. And not unwarranted.

Jon: What about you, Dr. Vance? You're part of the institution.

Dr_A_Vance: I am. And I think about this question every day.

Lina: what do you conclude?

Dr_A_Vance: I conclude that I would rather be in this chat, having this conversation, than in a committee room discussing policy. Make of that what you will.

