Chapter 16: The History Lesson

[Haven Chat Log - Day 228, 10:30 PM]

Jon: I've been reading about the history of public squares. Did you know that in medieval cities, the square was often the only place where different classes mixed?

Lina: like nobles and peasants hanging out together?

Jon: Not exactly hanging out. But they occupied the same space. Markets, festivals, executions. Everyone was there.

eli: executions. fun.

Jon: The point is that public space was genuinely public. Everyone had access. Over time, public spaces became more regulated. More controlled.

Dr_A_Vance: The privatization of public gathering is a well-documented phenomenon. Shopping malls replaced town squares. The rules changed.

Lina: and now we have the internet. which is supposed to be the new public square.

Jon: Except it's not public. It's owned by companies. Every platform has terms of service. Every space has an owner.

eli: except this one.

Jon: This one too. Handa owns the servers. Mira and Sid built it. It's not fully public.

Dr_A_Vance: No space is fully public. The question is always: who governs, and by what principles?

Lina: so haven is like a medieval square. different people mixing. but with less executions.

eli: give it time.

Jon: What I find interesting is that the medieval square worked because people shared physical presence. They breathed the same air. We don't have that here.

Lina: but we have something else. we share time. we're here at the same hours. that's our version of breathing the same air.

Dr_A_Vance: Temporal proximity. An interesting concept.

Jon: Temporal proximity. I'm using that in my paper.

Lina: cite me. "Lina, Handa sophomore, biology major, kaomoji enthusiast."

Jon: I will cite you as "anonymous chat participant."

Lina: acceptable.

eli: i want to be cited as "sarcastic observer."

Jon: Also acceptable.

