Chapter 27: The Impossible Export

[Haven Chat Log - Day 390, 11:30 PM]

eli: my discord is eli#0034. add me before it's too late or whatever.

Lina: eli.

eli: what. i'm being practical.

Lina: i know. it just feels weird.

Jon: Thank you, Eli. But I don't want to just be your Discord friend.

eli: what does that mean.

Jon: I want to be here, in this weird little box, at 2 AM, with you. The timestamps. The terrible emojis from Lina. The way you forget to use capital letters. That's the friendship I want. That one. The one that lives here.

[Timestamp: 11:35 PM]

Lina: you just made me cry into my keyboard.

eli: yeah, well. same.

Dr_A_Vance: I have been attempting to export the chat logs. The full archive. It is... substantial.

Jon: How substantial?

Dr_A_Vance: Five hundred and forty-seven days. Thousands of messages. The scrollback buffer alone is a novel.

Lina: we wrote a novel together and didn't even realize it.

eli: a terrible novel. full of typos and heating system analysis.

Lina: a beautiful novel.

Mira: sid is trying to build an export tool. so everyone can save their logs.

Sid: [Admin] it's almost done. you'll get a text file. every message you ever sent. every message sent to you.

Lina: sid. thank you.

Sid: [Admin] it's just code.

Mira: it's not just code.

Sid: [Admin] i know.

Jon: The export is important. But it won't be the same. Reading old messages in a text file is like reading the libretto without the music.

eli: that's very poetic. also very sad.

Jon: It's the truth. The context is the space. The space is the context.

Lina: so we can't save it. not really.

Dr_A_Vance: We can remember it. That is a form of saving.

eli: memories are unreliable.

Dr_A_Vance: So are chat logs. Time stamps erode. Servers fail. Memory and data are both imperfect vessels. But they are what we have.

Mira: then we fill both. while we still can.

